  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"123\" data-end=\"320\">The <strong data-start=\"127\" data-end=\"161\">Cryoglobulins Qualitative Test</strong> detects <strong data-start=\"170\" data-end=\"187\">cryoglobulins</strong>&mdash;abnormal proteins (usually immunoglobulins) that <strong data-start=\"237\" data-end=\"263\">precipitate (solidify)</strong> at cold temperatures and <strong data-start=\"289\" data-end=\"301\">dissolve</strong> again when warmed.</p>\n<p data-start=\"322\" data-end=\"343\">This test is used to:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"345\" data-end=\"641\">\n<li data-start=\"345\" data-end=\"422\">\n<p data-start=\"347\" data-end=\"422\">Investigate <strong data-start=\"359\" data-end=\"385\">unexplained vasculitis</strong>, purpura, joint pain, or neuropathy.</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"423\" data-end=\"542\">\n<p data-start=\"425\" data-end=\"542\">Diagnose <strong data-start=\"434\" data-end=\"454\">cryoglobulinemia</strong>, a condition associated with various infections, autoimmune diseases, and malignancies.</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"543\" data-end=\"641\">\n<p data-start=\"545\" data-end=\"641\">Evaluate patients with <strong data-start=\"568\" data-end=\"588\">cold sensitivity</strong>, <strong data-start=\"590\" data-end=\"614\">Raynaud&rsquo;s phenomenon</strong>, or <strong data-start=\"619\" data-end=\"640\">renal involvement</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"643\" data-end=\"665\">There are three types:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"666\" data-end=\"878\">\n<li data-start=\"666\" data-end=\"740\">\n<p data-start=\"668\" data-end=\"740\"><strong data-start=\"668\" data-end=\"678\">Type I</strong>: Usually monoclonal; linked to lymphoproliferative disorders.</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"741\" data-end=\"878\">\n<p data-start=\"743\" data-end=\"878\"><strong data-start=\"743\" data-end=\"787\">Type II and III (Mixed Cryoglobulinemia)</strong>: Associated with infections (especially hepatitis C), autoimmune diseases (e.g., SLE, RA).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"937\" data-end=\"1052\">A <strong data-start=\"939\" data-end=\"969\">positive cryoglobulin test</strong> indicates the presence of cryoglobulins in the blood. This may be associated with:</p>\n<div class=\"_tableContainer_80l1q_1\">\n<div class=\"_tableWrapper_80l1q_14 group flex w-fit flex-col-reverse\" tabindex=\"-1\">\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Explanation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hepatitis C infection</strong></td>\n<td>Most common cause of mixed cryoglobulinemia (Types II &amp; III)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Autoimmune diseases</strong></td>\n<td>Such as SLE or rheumatoid arthritis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Lymphoproliferative disorders</strong></td>\n<td>E.g., multiple myeloma, Waldenstr&ouml;m&rsquo;s macroglobulinemia (Type I)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Idiopathic cryoglobulinemia</strong></td>\n<td>No identifiable underlying condition</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
